course: world
lesson: 88
title: Recycling, waste and plastic
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Rubbish we throw away usually goes to one of three places. It can be buried in a landfill, burned in an incinerator that often makes electricity from the heat, or sorted and recycled into new materials. Rotting food in a landfill gives off methane, a gas that warms the planet.
explain: Recycling works best for some materials. Glass can be melted and made into new glass again and again. Recycling an aluminium can uses only about a twentieth of the energy needed to make a new one from rock. Paper can be recycled only a few times, because its fibres get shorter each time.
explain: At a recycling plant, machines sort the mixed rubbish. Big magnets pull out steel cans, while aluminium, which is not magnetic, is separated in other ways. Dirty or greasy things, like a pizza box soaked with oil, can spoil a whole load of paper.
explain: There are many kinds of plastic, and many of them are hard to recycle, so only a small share of all plastic ever made has been recycled. Plastic does not rot away; sunlight and waves break it into smaller and smaller pieces called microplastics, which are now found from deep oceans to Arctic snow.
explain: That is why reducing waste comes first, then reusing, and recycling last. A refillable water bottle used for a year saves hundreds of plastic bottles from ever being made.
fact: Landfills, incinerators and recycling plants are the main places rubbish goes.
fact: Food rotting in a landfill gives off methane, a gas that warms the planet.
fact: Food scraps rot into useful compost, but plastic does not rot.
fact: Glass can be recycled again and again without getting worse.
fact: Recycling aluminium cans saves about 95 percent of the energy needed to make new aluminium.
fact: Paper can be recycled only a few times because its fibres get shorter.
fact: Magnets are used at recycling plants to pull out steel cans.
fact: Greasy or dirty items can spoil a load of recycling.
fact: Many kinds of plastic are hard to recycle.
fact: Plastic breaks into tiny pieces called microplastics instead of rotting away.
fact: Old batteries and electronics must not go in the ordinary bin, because they can catch fire and hold harmful metals.
fact: Reducing and reusing save more than recycling does.
exercise: What are the three main places our rubbish goes? | Landfill, incinerator or recycling plant | The sea, the forest or the desert | The sky, the river or the mountains | Shops, schools or hospitals
exercise: Which gas comes from food rotting in a landfill? | Methane | Oxygen | Helium | Nitrogen
exercise: How can burning rubbish in an incinerator be useful? | The heat can be used to make electricity | The smoke makes the air cleaner | The ash turns back into new plastic | The fire makes the rubbish disappear with no waste
exercise: Which material can be recycled again and again without getting worse? | Glass | Paper | Cardboard | Most plastics
exercise: Why is it so worthwhile to recycle aluminium cans? | It saves most of the energy needed to make new ones | Aluminium is the cheapest metal to dig up | Aluminium cans rot quickly in the ground | Recycled cans are much lighter than new ones
exercise: Why can paper be recycled only a few times? | Its fibres get shorter each time | It turns into plastic after recycling | The ink can never be washed out | It becomes too heavy to use
exercise: How are steel cans pulled out of mixed rubbish? | With big magnets | With strong fans | With water tanks | With sticky rollers
exercise: Why should a greasy pizza box not go in the paper recycling? | The grease can spoil the recycled paper | The box is far too big for the sorting machines | Cardboard can never be recycled into anything | The grease makes the whole box turn magnetic
exercise: What happens to a plastic bottle left in the sea for many years? | It breaks into smaller and smaller pieces | It rots away completely in just a few weeks | It turns into soft sand that the fish can eat | It dissolves into clean, fresh salt water
exercise: What are microplastics? | Tiny pieces broken off larger plastic | Plastics that are made by tiny animals | Plastics that rot quickly in the soil | Small toys made from recycled plastic
exercise: Why should old batteries not go in the ordinary bin? | They can catch fire and contain harmful metals | They are too heavy for the bin lorry | They will charge themselves up again | They are made of glass that cuts the bags
exercise: Which saves the most resources? | Not making the waste in the first place | Recycling all of the waste carefully afterwards | Burning the waste in an incinerator for heat | Burying the waste deep in a landfill site
exercise: What happens to food scraps put in a compost heap? | They rot into rich soil for plants | They turn into plastic over time | They stay the same for hundreds of years | They give off gas that cleans the air
exercise: Why is a refillable water bottle better than buying bottled water? | It stops many plastic bottles from being made | It keeps water colder than any other bottle | Plastic bottles are easier to recycle than metal | Refillable bottles are made of paper and rot away
test: A class collects cans. The teacher holds a magnet near each can, and some stick. What does this show? | The cans that stick are steel and the others are not | The cans that stick are aluminium and the others are steel | The cans that stick are empty and the others are full | The cans that stick are clean and the others are dirty
test: Which is true about plastic in nature? | It breaks into pieces but does not rot away | It rots into rich garden soil within one year | It slowly turns back into crude oil in the ground | It is eaten up by hungry worms and disappears
test: A family wants to make less rubbish. Which change helps most? | Buying fewer things in plastic packaging | Putting all their rubbish in one big bag | Burning their rubbish in the garden | Throwing their rubbish into the river
test: Why do recycling centres ask people to rinse yoghurt pots before recycling them? | Dirty pots can spoil the clean recycled material | Rinsing makes the pots lighter to carry | Clean pots turn into glass much more easily later | Water helps the pots rot away faster
test: Scientists find tiny plastic pieces in snow near the North Pole, far from any city. How did they most likely get there? | Larger plastic broke up and the bits were carried by wind and sea | People in the Arctic make lots of plastic | Snow turns into plastic when it is very cold | Polar bears carry plastic bags there
test: What would happen if a landfill took only plastic and no food waste? | It would give off much less methane | It would give off much more methane | The plastic would rot away quickly | It would turn into compost in a year
test: Why is a glass jar a good thing to recycle? | Its glass can be made into new jars again and again | Glass rots away quickly if it is buried | Recycled glass becomes much lighter than new glass is | Glass can be recycled only once or twice
test: An old phone stops working. What is the best thing to do with it? | Take it to a special electronics recycling point | Throw it in the ordinary kitchen bin | Bury it in the garden | Throw it in the paper recycling
test: Which is the right order, from what helps most to what helps least? | Reduce, then reuse, then recycle | Recycle, then reuse, then reduce | Reuse, then recycle, then reduce | Recycle, then reduce, then reuse
test: why can't u chuck old batteries in the normal bin | They can catch fire and hold harmful metals | They are way too heavy for the bin lorry to lift | They make all the other rubbish rot much faster | They will recharge themselves and waste power
test: what's better, recycling stuff or just using less | Reducing and reusing save more than recycling | Recycling always saves the most, way more than anything | They both save exactly the same | Throwing things away saves the most
test: Leo says plastic rots away in a few years like food. true? | No, plastic breaks into tiny microplastics instead of rotting | Yes, plastic rots into compost quite quickly | Yes, but only in a hot sunny place | No, plastic stays whole and never breaks up
test: my sister reckons paper can be recycled forever. right? | No, only a few times, because its fibres get shorter | Yes, paper never gets worse however many times it's recycled | Yes, just like glass and metal | No, paper can never be recycled at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the recycling of glass? | It may be recycled again and again without getting worse | It may be recycled only once | It cannot be recycled at all | It becomes weaker with every recycling
exam: Identify the principal reason for which paper may be recycled only a few times. | Its fibres become shorter on each occasion | Its ink can never be removed | It turns into a kind of plastic when it is recycled | It becomes heavier on each occasion
exam: Ascertain the means by which steel cans are separated at recycling plants. | By magnets | By water | By wind | By hand alone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the fate of plastic in the environment. | It breaks into tiny pieces called microplastics instead of rotting | It rots into useful compost within a year | It dissolves wholly in rain water | It turns into glass in sunlight
